  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nHigh pressure to our north will remain a solid influence over\nour weather pattern through the weekend and into next week. Dry\nweather is anticipated for the next seven days as a result.\nTemperatures will be fairly moderate and comfortable over the\nnext week. High temperatures each afternoon will be around to\nslightly above the seasonable normal for the area with the\nlowlands reaching the mid 60s to lower 70s each day and the \nmountains staying in the 50s to mid 60s.\n\nLow temperatures each night will drop into the 30s to mid 40s.\nThe lower end of the range will be more common across the\nmountains, but these lower temperatures could creep into the\nnorthern and central lowlands Saturday and Sunday nights leading\nto some areas of patchy frost. The growing season is still\nactive across the lowlands. Dense fog is expected to form each \nmorning, particularly in the river valley and sheltered mountain\nvalleys.\n\nDew points will be fairly dry each afternoon with 30s across the\nmountains and 40s in the lowlands. Relative humidity values will\nbe in the comfortable to slightly dry range (40 to 60%) Friday\nand Saturday with a few valleys reaching the 20s and 30s. Warmer\ntemperatures Sunday and Monday will cause drier RH values in the\n30s and 20s across a decent chunk of the lowlands and mountain\nvalleys. Recovery is expected each night though and winds will\nbe calm to light through the period so fire weather concerns are\nlow at this time.\n\nA slow moving front will cross the area mid week and stall \nahead of a cold front that will move through late next week. \nCurrently, rain chances do not look to return until next weekend\nwith the crossing of the cold front. However, fleeting chances\nfor isolated showers are possible Thursday into Friday.",
